  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We propose a hybrid optimization strategy combining gradient-based methods with evolutionary search to address the non-convex nature of the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The key intuition is that convex relaxations may not be sufficient to escape local minima in this complex optimization landscape. Our approach features:\n   - A coarse-to-fine resolution strategy starting from a smaller grid and progressively increasing n_points\n   - Evolutionary phase with simulated annealing to explore the solution space\n   - Local refinement using projected gradient descent\n   - Dynamic dimensionality adaptation\n   The method specifically targets the gap in performance seen in the parent implementation by introducing:\n   1. A novel smooth surrogate objective function that better captures the optimization landscape\n   2. Dimensionally-scaled optimization parameters to handle varying grid sizes\n   3. Progressive repair mechanisms to maintain solution feasibility during optimization\n   The approach leverages the provided initial_h_values while respecting Erd\u0151s's constraint structure through careful projection techniques.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Variables: Step function h(x) over [0,2] with integral constraint\n   - Objective: min max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx using finite-sample correlation\n   - Constraints: 0\u2264h(x)\u22641 and \u222b h(x) dx =1\n   - Representation: Discrete grid of n_points samples with spacing 2/n_points\n   - Formulation:\n     \u2022 Smooth surrogate: exp(-\u03b1(c_m - log(sum(exp(c_shift)))/\u03b2)\n     \u2022 Projection: Iterative clipping with volume preservation\n     \u2022 Fitness: Gradient information from correlation lags\n   - Optimization strategy:\n     \u2022 Initial coarse grid: Initialize from initial_h_values or uniform distribution\n     \u2022 Progressive resolution: Double grid size until deadline or budget expires\n     \u2022 Multistage optimization: Coevolution with simulated annealing\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   a. Initialization:\n      - Start with n = len(initial_h_values) if available, else n = 100\n      - h_start = initial_h_values.copy() if available, else np.linspace(0,2,100) evaluated with uniform weights\n      - Project to feasible set via project_feasible()\n   b. Progressive resolution:\n      - For each grid doubling (doubling n until deadline):\n        - Compute all lags with np.correlate(mode='full')\n        - Terminate if deadline approached\n        - Apply two-phase optimization:\n          Phase I: Gradient-based refinement with many iterations\n          Phase II: Evolutionary search with adaptive grid repair\n   c. Objective evaluation:\n      - compute_c5(h, n): calculates maximum overlap integral scaled dx\n      - compute_all_lags(h,n): computes entire correlation array scaled dx\n   d. Optimization methods:\n      Phase I (gradient-based):\n        - Projected gradient descent on smooth surrogate\n        - Warm start from previous grid resolution\n        - Constraint restoration via volume-preserving clipping\n      Phase II (evolutionary):\n        - Simulated annealing with grid-aware mutation\n        - Temperature schedule based on grid resolution\n        - Geometric crossover maintaining integral constraint\n   e. Termination criteria:\n      - Update every 10 grid expansions\n      - Track best solution across all grid sizes\n      - Stop when time budget exhausted\n      - Maintain minimal feasible solution\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   a. Primary variation: Progressive resolution grid expansion factor \u03b1=2\n   b. Parameter scaling:\n      \u2022 Optimization iterations: 50 for base grid, halved per doubling\n      \u2022 Iteration count: 300 for base grid, reduced by 20% per doubling\n      \u2022 Cooling rate: 0.98 for base grid, decreased by 0.8% per doubling\n   c. Adaptive repair:\n      - Volume preserving adjustment upon constraint violation\n      - Neighborhood sample replacement for stuck particles\n   d. Fallback mechanism:\n      - After convergence failure, try scale transformation h' = (h - min) / (max-min) * new_bounds\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   a. Time budget estimation:\n      \u2022 Base grid (n=100): ~10% of total time\n      \u2022 Each doubling: ~20% less computation budget\n      \u2022 Total grid doublings: ~3 during 1100s budget\n   b. Deadline handling:\n      \u2022 Time monitor: Break inner loops when deadline < 0.5s\n      \u2022 Progressive early termination: Reduce operation counts when approaching deadline\n      \u2022 Minimal repair: Only maintain feasibility without full convergence\n   c. Numerical stability:\n      - Clip extreme values but maintain integral via volume preserving\n      - Use log-sum-exp in surrogate objectives\n      - Scale covariance matrices by grid resolution\n   d. Validation checks:\n      - Always validate solution bounds and integral\n      - Re-evaluate best solution at end with exact metric\n      - Maintain history of best solutions per grid resolution\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   a. Input validation:\n      - Check n_points parameter bounds (auto)\n      - Verify initial_h_values constraints\n      - Initialize with minimal grid for speed\n   b. Feasibility checks:\n      - All solutions: clip h[i] \u2208 [0,1], project to sum=n/2\n      - Evaluate objective only for feasible solutions\n   c. Output contract:\n      - Return (h_best, c5_bound, n_points)\n      - c5_bound must be float\n      - h_best must be array of floats of dimension n_points\n      - sum(h_best) must equal n_points/2\n      - Project_feasible(h_best) must \u2208 [0,1] with sum unchanged\n      - c5_bound < current record 0.3808\n   d. Minimal objective evaluation:\n      - Use np.correlate(mode='full') with scaling dx=2/n_points\n      - No autoregressive assumptions\n      - No circular correlation",
